# The table scene: column headers + click-to-sort on the For
# vocabulary (docs/tables-plan.md). column#0 is the For container (the
# root is a row, the reorder scene's rule). expect_columns reads the
# header bar AS THE TABLE PATH PRESENTED IT — titles in visual order,
# the indicator as ^N (asc) or vN (desc); HEADERS RENDER AT EVERY
# WIDTH (ratified 2026-08-21: the compact degrade died — nothing but
# an Apple-idiom analogy ever forced it, and it cost the phones their
# headers and this scene its phone legs). expect_rows reads per-row
# cell labels in the toolkit's child order, rows joined with `|`,
# cells with `,` — the two-level expect_order, because a
# creation-order registry cannot see the moves a sort makes.
# expect_column_edges is the GEOMETRY claim every backend makes
# uniformly, both halves: header cell i and every row's cell i share
# a leading edge with exactly N such edges, AND the table spans its
# assigned track — a column's content is its width FLOOR, leftover
# distributes, the table fills its viewport (decision 6; mac's native
# columns add user resize on top, which is the one affordance the
# synthesized tiers lack). A header click is a REQUEST: the platform sorts nothing;
# the guest reorders by key and re-declares the indicator, so every
# expectation below is the guest's policy (same column flips, a new
# column starts ascending) coming back through the toolkit.
expect_columns column#0 "Name|Size"
expect_column_edges column#0 2
# A table is a viewport: its rows may leave slack, but they may not
# extend beyond the track the grown For received.
expect_fills column#0
expect_rows column#0 "banana,30|apple,10|cherry,20"
header_click column#0 0
expect_columns column#0 "Name|Size ^0"
expect_column_edges column#0 2
expect_rows column#0 "apple,10|banana,30|cherry,20"
header_click column#0 0
expect_columns column#0 "Name|Size v0"
expect_column_edges column#0 2
expect_rows column#0 "cherry,20|banana,30|apple,10"
header_click column#0 1
expect_columns column#0 "Name|Size ^1"
expect_column_edges column#0 2
expect_rows column#0 "apple,10|cherry,20|banana,30"
